<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samguk yusa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1280s</span> · Also known as Memorabilia of the Three Kingdoms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Samguk yusa, or Memorabilia of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms</a>, is a collection of Korean histories, legends and Buddhist tales compiled by the monk Iryeon in the 1280s. It preserves material missing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>, including the foundation myth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dangun">Dangun</a> and the traditions of the Gaya states. It is a key source for early Korean history and folklore.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa.jpg" alt="Samguk yusa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salamander724 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memorabilia_of_the_Three_Kingdoms_in_museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
